from lib import *
S='social-science'
lesson(10,S,'history-the-world-between-two-world-wars','depression-fascism-and-nazism',"The Great Depression and the rise of Fascism and Nazism","When money stopped moving","On 24 October 1929 share prices in the United States collapsed. Shareholders rushed to sell, nobody wanted to buy, banks failed, and the shock travelled across the world. Within a few years millions had lost their jobs, and in several countries angry people turned to leaders who promised strength and order.",
["The First World War left Europe weakened and in debt, and the Treaty of Versailles added political complications and a wave of economic nationalism with tariff barriers. @ after the war",
 "The first huge Stock Market Crash in the USA came on 24 October 1929; American banks failed and financiers withdrew funds invested abroad. @ crash of 1929",
 "England left the Gold Standard, many countries followed, and each adopted protectionism and devaluation, so world trade and credit contracted sharply: the Great Depression. @ Gold Standard",
 "The Depression changed politics: Labour lost in England in 1931 and the Republicans were rejected in the USA for about twenty years. @ political effects",
 "In Italy, unhappy ex-soldiers, industrialists and the middle classes backed Mussolini's Fascist Party, which stood for authority, strength and discipline and freely used violence. @ Mussolini and Fascism",
 "In October 1922 Mussolini organised the Fascist March on Rome and the King invited him to form a government. @ March on Rome 1922",
 "Fascism is radical authoritarian ultra-nationalism with dictatorial power and suppression of opposition; in Germany a similar movement, Nazism under Hitler, grew from the humiliation of Versailles and the Depression. @ Fascism and Nazism"],
("A country is hit by a depression, and people blame the government and an old treaty. Why might they turn to a strong leader?",[("Hunger and unemployment make people desperate for quick answers.","Economic pain feeds anger."),("Strong leaders promise order, jobs and national pride, as Mussolini and Hitler did.","That promise attracted support."),("The cost is the loss of freedom and the suppression of opposition.","Dictatorship follows.")]),
("Explain how the Great Depression helped dictators rise.",["Crash of 1929 spread worldwide","Unemployment and anger at Versailles","Fascists and Nazis promised strength and order"],["Saying the Depression was only an American event"]),
["Great Depression began with the crash of 24 October 1929.","Countries left the Gold Standard and raised tariffs.","Mussolini came to power after the March on Rome, 1922.","Fascism: ultra-nationalist dictatorship; Nazism in Germany."],
["m|The Stock Market Crash that began the Great Depression happened in|1929|1914~Start of World War I;1939~Start of World War II|24 October 1929.",
 "t|Many countries left the Gold Standard during the Depression.|True|England left first and others followed.",
 "n|The year of Mussolini's March on Rome|1922|October 1922.",
 "m|Fascism is best described as|ultra-nationalist dictatorship|rule by elected workers' councils~That describes Soviet ideas;a type of free-trade system~It was the opposite|Fascists suppressed opposition.",
 "t|Protectionism and devaluation helped revive world trade at once.|False|They reduced trade and credit.",
 "s|The Italian leader who founded the Fascist movement was ____.|Mussolini|He led Italy from 1922.",
 "t|The Treaty of Versailles added to the political complications after the war.|True|Its harsh terms caused resentment.",
 "m|Who stood for authority, strength and discipline in Italy?|The Fascists|The Socialists~They opposed the war|The Liberals~They lost ground|Their slogan attracted nationalists and ex-soldiers."])
lesson(10,S,'history-world-war-ii','causes-course-and-results',"World War II: causes, course and results","A war that covered the globe","Six years after Germany invaded Poland, the world was in ruins. Cities were bombed, millions had died and two new bombs showed what science could now destroy. Out of this war came the United Nations and a new map of power.",
["Causes included the unjust Treaty of Versailles, the Great Depression, the rise of Fascist and Nazi dictators, the failure of the League of Nations and the policy of appeasement. @ causes",
 "World War II began on 1 September 1939 when Germany invaded Poland and Britain and France declared war. @ 1 September 1939",
 "The Axis powers were Germany, Italy and Japan; the Allies included Britain, France, the Soviet Union and later the USA. @ Axis and Allies",
 "Japan attacked Pearl Harbor in December 1941, which brought the United States into the war. @ Pearl Harbor 1941",
 "Germany surrendered in May 1945; the war with Japan ended in August 1945 after the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki. @ 1945 ends the war",
 "The war killed tens of millions, destroyed cities and economies, and ended the dominance of the old European colonial empires. @ results",
 "The United Nations was founded in 1945 to maintain peace, replacing the League of Nations. @ United Nations"],
("Why did the League of Nations fail to stop a second world war, while the United Nations was created in 1945?",[("The League had no army and the USA never joined, so aggressors ignored it.","It lacked real power."),("The UN was built with a Security Council of great powers to act together.","It tried to fix that weakness.")]),
("Give two causes and two results of World War II.",["Versailles resentment and dictators","Appeasement and failure of League","Atomic bombs and end of war in 1945","United Nations formed"],["Mixing up the dates of the two wars"]),
["War began 1 September 1939 with the attack on Poland.","Axis: Germany, Italy, Japan; Allies: Britain, France, USSR, USA.","War ended in 1945; atomic bombs dropped on Japan.","United Nations formed in 1945."],
["n|The year World War II began|1939|Germany invaded Poland on 1 September 1939.",
 "m|Which powers formed the Axis?|Germany, Italy and Japan|Britain, France and the USA~They were Allies;Russia, China and India~Not the Axis|Axis powers.",
 "t|The Pearl Harbor attack brought the USA into the war.|True|Japan attacked in December 1941.",
 "n|The year the Second World War ended|1945|Germany in May and Japan in August 1945.",
 "t|The League of Nations was created after World War II.|False|It was created after World War I; the UN followed World War II.",
 "s|The international organisation formed in 1945 was the ____ Nations.|United|The UN was founded in 1945.",
 "m|Appeasement meant|giving in to aggressive demands to avoid war|attacking first~Opposite|joining the Axis~Wrong side|It encouraged dictators.",
 "t|The war strengthened European colonial empires.|False|It weakened them and led to independence of colonies."])
lesson(10,S,'history-the-world-after-world-war-ii','cold-war-and-chinese-revolution',"The Cold War, Marshall Plan and the Chinese Revolution","Two giants and one divided world","After 1945 two super powers, the USA and the USSR, faced each other without a direct war, and the world split into two blocs. At the same time the old empires were fading and, in China, a revolution changed the largest country in Asia.",
["The Second World War ended the era of European colonial empires and left the USA and the USSR as the two super powers, dividing the world into antagonistic blocs. @ two super powers",
 "This tension without direct war between the blocs is the Cold War, which triggered conflicts in Korea, Cuba, Vietnam and West Asia and ended with the fall of the Berlin Wall. @ Cold War",
 "Under the Marshall Plan the USA helped rebuild war-ravaged Europe, while the USSR won goodwill by supporting liberation struggles in Asia and Africa. @ Marshall Plan",
 "The Non-Aligned Movement played a limited role in containing the conflict between the two power blocs. @ Non-Aligned Movement",
 "In China the Manchu dynasty had ruled since about 1650; peasants suffered high rents and the Opium Wars forced China to open its ports to western powers. @ old China",
 "Discontent led to uprisings like the Taiping Rebellion (1850-64); in 1911 the army mutinied, the Manchu rule fell and Dr Sun Yat-sen became provisional president of the new Republic. @ Sun Yat-sen",
 "The Second World War helped bring the communist revolution in China, after which the People's Republic was proclaimed in 1949 under Mao Zedong. @ Communist China"],
("A weak ruler, poor peasants and foreign powers controlling ports: what kind of situation produces a revolution?",[("Poverty and high rents anger the peasants.","The mass of people suffers."),("Foreign domination hurts national pride.","It adds hatred of outsiders."),("A new leader or party offers change, as Sun Yat-sen and later the Communists did.","Leadership gives direction.")]),
("What was the Cold War and what was the Marshall Plan?",["Rivalry of USA and USSR without direct war","Division into two blocs","Marshall Plan rebuilt Europe"],["Saying the Cold War was fought directly between the two powers"]),
["After 1945: USA and USSR as two super powers.","Cold War: rivalry without a direct war; ended with fall of the Berlin Wall.","Marshall Plan: US aid to rebuild Europe.","China: Manchu rule ended in 1911; People's Republic in 1949."],
["m|The two super powers after World War II were|the USA and the USSR|Britain and France~They were weakened;Japan and Germany~They were defeated|Both led blocs.",
 "t|The Cold War was a direct full-scale war between the USA and USSR.|False|It was rivalry short of direct war.",
 "m|The Marshall Plan was meant to|rebuild war-ravaged Europe|start a new war~Opposite|colonise Asia~Wrong|It was US aid.",
 "n|The year the Manchu dynasty fell in China|1911|The revolution of 1911 ended Manchu rule.",
 "t|Dr Sun Yat-sen became the first provisional president of the Chinese Republic.|True|He was elected after the 1911 uprising.",
 "s|The Cold War period ended with the fall of the ____ Wall.|Berlin|Symbol of the divided Europe.",
 "t|The Non-Aligned Movement completely ended the conflict between the blocs.|False|It played only a limited role.",
 "m|The Taiping Rebellion lasted|1850-64|1900-14~Not it;1750-64~Earlier|A major uprising against the Manchus."])
lesson(10,S,'history-social-and-religious-reform-movements-in-the-19th-century','reformers-and-reform-societies',"19th century reformers: Vidyasagar, Arya Samaj, Prarthana Samaj","Voices against old customs","A child widow, a ban on remarriage, a custom nobody dared question: in the 19th century some Indians began to ask whether these practices were really part of their faith. Education, printing and new ideas gave them the courage to speak and to build societies for change.",
["Iswar Chandra Vidyasagar campaigned for the remarriage of widows, which led to the Widows' Remarriage Act of 1856, and he promoted the education of girls. @ Vidyasagar",
 "The first age of consent was included in the Indian Penal Code of 1860 and the marriage age was later raised, but the law often remained on paper. @ age of consent",
 "Swami Dayanand Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in 1875 in Punjab; his book Satyarthaprakash spread its ideas and his cry was go back to the Vedas. @ Arya Samaj 1875",
 "He rejected child marriage, idolatry and Brahman domination of ritual, and stood for strict monotheism. @ principles",
 "Arya Samaj prescribed a purificatory ceremony called suddhi for Hindus who had converted. @ suddhi",
 "The Prarthana Samaj was founded in Bombay in 1867 by Dr Atma Ram Pandurang; R.C. Bhandarkar and Justice M.G. Ranade worked for inter-caste dining, inter-caste marriage, widow remarriage and women's improvement. @ Prarthana Samaj 1867",
 "These movements combined reason, education and social service to reform society and prepared the ground for later nationalism. @ legacy"],
("Why did reformers insist that a custom like banning widow remarriage had no support in scripture?",[("People defend customs by saying they are sacred.","Authority of tradition."),("Showing that the old texts do not require it removes the excuse.","That was Dayanand's and Vidyasagar's argument."),("Then law and education can change the practice.","Reform needs both ideas and law.")]),
("Name two reformers and one reform they achieved.",["Vidyasagar and the Widows' Remarriage Act 1856","Dayanand and Arya Samaj 1875","Prarthana Samaj and Ranade"],["Confusing Arya Samaj with Brahmo Samaj"]),
["Vidyasagar: widow remarriage law of 1856, girls' schools.","Arya Samaj: Dayanand Saraswati, 1875, Satyarthaprakash.","Prarthana Samaj: Bombay, 1867.","Reform needed both new ideas and new laws."],
["n|The year of the Widows' Remarriage Act|1856|Passed due to Vidyasagar's campaign.",
 "m|Who founded the Arya Samaj?|Swami Dayanand Saraswati|Raja Rammohan Roy~He founded the Brahmo Samaj;Dr Atma Ram Pandurang~He founded Prarthana Samaj|Founded in 1875.",
 "t|The Prarthana Samaj was founded in Bombay.|True|It began in 1867.",
 "n|The year the Arya Samaj was founded|1875|In Punjab.",
 "t|Suddhi was a purificatory ceremony of the Arya Samaj.|True|Meant for those who had converted.",
 "s|Dayanand's book was the ____.|Satyarthaprakash|It spread Arya Samaj ideas.",
 "m|Which reformer promoted girls' education and widow remarriage?|Vidyasagar|Ranade~He worked in Maharashtra too but the law is linked with Vidyasagar|Dayanand~Founded Arya Samaj|Pioneer of modern Bengali prose.",
 "t|The law on age of consent was always strictly followed.|False|The Age of Consent Committee found it largely stayed on paper."])
lesson(10,S,'history-early-revolts-against-british-rule-in-tamil-nadu','palayakkarars-wars',"Palayakkarars and the first resistance in Tamil Nadu","Chiefs who said no","After the Carnatic Wars the East India Company began to extend its power in the south. The local chieftains, called Palayakkarars, did not accept it quietly. Their wars are the first chapter of resistance to British rule in Tamil Nadu.",
["A palayam means a domain or little kingdom; the system was introduced in Tamil Nadu by Viswanatha Nayaka of Madurai in 1529 with his minister Ariyanathar. @ palayam system 1529",
 "Traditionally there were 72 Palayakkarars who collected revenue, administered territory, settled disputes and kept law and order through Padikaval or Arasu Kaval. @ 72 Palayakkarars",
 "The system lasted about two hundred years until the British took over; eastern palayams included Sattur, Ettayapuram and Panchalankurichi, western ones Uthumalai and Singampatti. @ eastern and western palayams",
 "The first resistance came from Puli Thevar of Nerkattumseval, who tried to get help from Hyder Ali and the French. @ Puli Thevar",
 "Other leaders followed: Velunachiyar, Veerapandiya Kattabomman, the Marudhu brothers and Dheeran Chinnamalai. @ later chieftains",
 "These Palayakkarars Wars were crushed by the Company's better arms and organisation, but they kept the spirit of resistance alive. @ result",
 "The struggle culminated in the Vellore Revolt of 1806. @ Vellore 1806"],
("Why could the British defeat the Palayakkarars even though they fought bravely?",[("The chieftains were divided and sometimes fought each other.","Lack of unity."),("The Company had modern firearms, cannons and a trained army.","Better weapons and organisation."),("Help from Hyder Ali or the French did not arrive in time.","No strong ally.")]),
("Who were the Palayakkarars and why are they remembered?",["Chieftains with palayams under the Nayaks","First to resist the Company in Tamil Nadu","Puli Thevar, Kattabomman, Marudhu brothers"],["Thinking they were British officers"]),
["Palayam system introduced by Viswanatha Nayaka in 1529.","72 Palayakkarars looked after revenue and law and order.","Puli Thevar was the first to resist the Company.","Resistance culminated in the Vellore Revolt of 1806."],
["m|The palayam system was introduced in Tamil Nadu by|Viswanatha Nayaka|Hyder Ali~He ruled Mysore;Robert Clive~A British leader|He was the Nayak ruler of Madurai.",
 "n|Traditional number of Palayakkarars|72|There were said to be 72 palayams.",
 "t|Puli Thevar was the first to resist the Company's expansion in the Tamil country.|True|He was from Nerkattumseval.",
 "n|The year of the Vellore Revolt|1806|It ended the early resistance period.",
 "t|Padikaval meant the revenue collection of the Palayakkarars.|False|It meant police and watch duties.",
 "s|The word palayam means a domain or a little ____.|kingdom|It also means a military camp.",
 "m|Which of these is NOT a Palayakkarar leader?|Robert Clive|Veerapandiya Kattabomman~He resisted the British|Marudhu brothers~They fought the British|Clive was a Company commander.",
 "t|The Palayakkarar system lasted about two hundred years.|True|Until the British took over."])
